  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study's findings are based on data from a single French city, which may limit their generalizability to different bioclimatic zones or highly dissimilar urban structures.",
      "what": "This study analyzed how urban heat islands, serving as a proxy for intense urbanization, affect the taxonomic, phylogenetic, and functional diversity of birds and bats in Poitiers, France. The researchers measured community composition, species turnover, and nestedness to determine how these flying vertebrates respond to urban heat gradients.",
      "why": "The findings reveal that bats and birds respond differently to urban heat, with bats experiencing a broad decline across all diversity metrics driven by nestedness, while birds show only taxonomic declines driven by species turnover. This distinction demonstrates that urban conservation strategies cannot treat all flying vertebrates as a single ecological group and must account for taxon-specific vulnerabilities to heat."
    }
  },
